//
// @C135 — loft has no unsafe construct: no loft code can observe how a value is represented
// (an address, a raw buffer, a layout), so the compiler may choose any representation that
// answers the same (`(R-Escape)`, C122).  Representation is observable at exactly two
// boundaries: a package's native code, and a store whose bytes persist or are mapped from a
// file — there the store layout IS the contract, read back by another run or tool.
//
// Each refusal below is a door that would hand a program a representation: an `unsafe`
// block, and a Rust body written into a program (`#rust` is the standard library's own
// template, C87; a package binds Rust with `#native` and its native crate).  A build that
// opens either door breaks every representation rewrite at once, silently, so this file
// fails first.  The file-backed store boundary is held by the store format's own guards
// (`tests/store_durable_format.rs`), not here.
//
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/c135-representation-is-observable-only-at-a-boundary.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
//   CHANNEL: the exit code, on both backends.  WITNESS: the patch lets a program's `#rust` body
//   through the parser, so the expected refusal is not produced and the run answers
//   differently.  HOLDS: no leak, panic or refusal on either tree.
//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Expect token ;
// @EXPECT_ERROR: #rust is the standard library's own Rust template, not a library route

fn bump(x: integer) -> integer;
#rust"@x + 1"

fn main() {
  v = [1, 2, 3];
  unsafe { println("{v}"); }
  println("{bump(len(v))}");
}
